  <!-- ===================================================================================
       formmaps#36: make an unproductive run END, with a name attached, instead of sitting
       there. `dotnet test` on this project prints NOTHING until the final summary, so a run
       that has stopped completing tests is indistinguishable from one that is merely slow.
       #36 was filed after ~50 minutes of exactly that.

       MEASURED, 2026-08-10, this project, no-build, whole project in one process:
         - mean duration of tests that build an ASP.NET host climbed 4.0 s -> 50.2 s over the
           run (buckets of 200 completions), while tests that only touch Postgres stayed FLAT
           at ~30 ms. The cost is host construction, not any fixture and not the database.
         - a serial control loop measured host build + one request at 2.0 s (10th host) rising
           to 3.8 s (290th). xunit runs collections 12-wide, so 12 concurrent builds of a
           2-4 s CPU-bound thing is where the ~50 s per test comes from.
         - the run stopped completing tests entirely, and the 7 tests in flight at that moment
           were in 7 DIFFERENT namespaces and all 7 were host-building endpoint tests. There is
           no single culprit class to fix.

       So there is no small change that makes ~2,470 tests in ONE process fast == that is what
       the sharded `integration` matrix in .github/workflows/formmaps-api-ci.yml is for. What
       these two properties buy is that the unsharded run TERMINATES and says something:
       vstest aborts and writes a Sequence_*.xml naming the tests that were in flight. That
       Sequence.xml is what identified the 7-namespace spread above. A hang carries no
       information; an abort carries the list.

       TIMEOUT SIZING. 10 minutes is inactivity: the clock resets on every completed test,
       so it does not cap the run, only a run that has stopped producing results. The slowest
       single legitimate test observed on a loaded box was 58 s, and
       RealtimeTicketEndpointTests deliberately sleeps ~65 s of wall clock, so 10 minutes is
       ~9x the worst honest case. It also lands well inside the 30-minute shard cap in
       formmaps-api-ci.yml, which matters: hitting the cap kills the job with no artifact,
       hitting this writes the Sequence.xml first.

       DUMP TYPE none IS DELIBERATE. The testhost carries >1 GB of working set by then, so a
       full hang dump is a multi-gigabyte CI artifact. Sequence.xml is emitted either way and
       is the part that names the tests.

       AssemblyMetadata below is what lets ContainerStartupTimeoutTests assert these survived,
       since MSBuild properties are otherwise invisible at runtime.
       =================================================================================== -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <VSTestBlameHang>true</VSTestBlameHang>
    <VSTestBlameHangTimeout>10m</VSTestBlameHangTimeout>
    <VSTestBlameHangDumpType>none</VSTestBlameHangDumpType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <AssemblyMetadata Include="FormMapsBlameHangTimeout" Value="$(VSTestBlameHangTimeout)" />
  </ItemGroup>

